      "lyrics": "Verse 1\nThe last chord dies like thunder in a cathedral of chrome\nTwenty thousand hands are waving, but I\u2019m walking back alone\nMascara on the mirror, sweat still running down my face\nThere\u2019s a champagne moon in the ice bucket\nAnd a Bible by the ashtray\n\nThe hallway hums with strangers, the elevator sighs\nEvery door\u2019s a secret with a do-not-disturb disguise\nI hear our song on someone\u2019s radio three floors below\nFunny how the higher you climb\nThe farther there is to go\n\nPre-Chorus\nWe were kings for ninety minutes\nBurning brighter than the sun\nNow the lights come down around us\nAnd the silence weighs a ton\n\nChorus\nEmpty rooms and sold-out signs\nNeon ghosts on the county lines\nWe gave our hearts to the roar of the crowd\nNow we can\u2019t hear our own hearts beating loud\nEmpty rooms and sold-out signs\nLiving the dream one goodbye at a time\nEverybody wants a piece of the flame\nBut nobody calls you by your name\nEmpty rooms and sold-out signs\n\nVerse 2\nThere\u2019s a picture from Ohio taped inside my guitar case\nFour broke boys in denim jackets with forever on their face\nNow the phones keep ringing business, and the lawyers know our songs\nWe got gold upon the walls\nBut we can\u2019t tell right from wrong\n\nThe city looks like diamonds from a penthouse in the rain\nBut diamonds cut you open when you hold \u2019em through the pain\nI dialed her number three times, let it ring, then let it die\nWhat do you say to someone you loved\nWhen you traded love for sky?\n\nPre-Chorus\nWe were kings for ninety minutes\nWith a spotlight for a crown\nThen the limo door swings open\nAnd the whole world shuts you out\n\nChorus\nEmpty rooms and sold-out signs\nNeon ghosts on the county lines\nWe gave our hearts to the roar of the crowd\nNow we can\u2019t hear our own hearts beating loud\nEmpty rooms and sold-out signs\nLiving the dream one goodbye at a time\nEverybody wants a piece of the flame\nBut nobody calls you by your name\nEmpty rooms and sold-out signs\n\nBridge\nOh, we laughed in the van when the heater broke down\nShared one cigarette on the edge of town\nSang to the stars like they owed us a chance\nNow we\u2019re million-mile boys in a slow hotel dance\n\nIf fame is a fire, then love is the rain\nIf you lose who you are, what the hell did you gain?\nSo I\u2019m raising this glass to the kids that we were\nBefore every encore started feeling like work\n\nFinal Chorus\nEmpty rooms and sold-out signs\nNeon ghosts on the county lines\nWe gave our hearts to the roar of the crowd\nNow we can\u2019t hear our own hearts beating loud\nEmpty rooms and sold-out signs\nLiving the dream one goodbye at a time\nEverybody wants a piece of the flame\nBut nobody calls you by your name\n\nEmpty rooms and sold-out signs\nWe shine like stars while we\u2019re falling behind\nAll of the glory, all of the noise\nCan\u2019t bring back the hometown boys\nEmpty rooms and sold-out signs\nEmpty rooms and sold-out signs\n\nOutro\nThe last bus leaves at midnight\nThe marquee burns our name\nFrom the window it looks beautiful\nBut it never feels the same\n\nEmpty rooms\nSold-out signs\nEmpty rooms\nSold-out signs",
